---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {timingExperiment,offerExperiment,bonusExperiment} from '../../lib/worked-experiments.mjs';
import '../../styles/experiments.css';
const timing=timingExperiment(), offers=offerExperiment(), bonus=bonusExperiment();
const money=(n:number)=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-AU',{style:'currency',currency:'AUD'}).format(n);
const shortfall=Math.max(0,-timing.original.minimumClosingBalance.amountCents)/100;
---
<Base title="Money experiments: three decisions you can check" description="Three original Howloop worked investigations: rent before payday, salary packages versus cash pay, and missed savings bonus conditions. Inspect the inputs, results and downloadable evidence.">
<div class="experiment-wrap hl-wrap">
<header class="experiment-hero"><p class="experiment-eyebrow">THE HOWLOOP MONEY LAB · 01</p><h1>The headline is only<br/>half the story.</h1><p class="experiment-lead">A good-looking balance. A bigger salary. A higher rate.<br/>Three small experiments show what the headline leaves out.</p><p>Constructed examples by Howloop · Published 17 October 2026 · All amounts AUD</p><nav aria-label="Choose an experiment"><a href="#timing">01 · Follow the dates</a><a href="#offers">02 · Unpack the offer</a><a href="#bonus">03 · Test the conditions</a></nav></header>
<aside class="experiment-note"><strong>Real calculations, imaginary situations.</strong> These are reproducible scenarios we designed, not customer stories, personal experience or product recommendations. Change your own inputs in the linked tools. <a href="/worked-examples/evidence.json" download="howloop-experiments.json">Download all default inputs and results (JSON)</a>.</aside>
<section class="experiment" id="timing"><p class="experiment-eyebrow">01 / FOLLOW THE DATES</p><h2>Can a positive budget still run out of money?</h2><p class="experiment-intro">Yes. This plan finishes with {money(timing.original.closingBalanceCents/100)}, but drops to {money(timing.original.minimumClosingBalance.amountCents/100)} before the first payday. A total cannot tell you when money is available.</p>
<div class="experiment-score"><div><span>Balance after 13 weeks</span><strong>{money(timing.original.closingBalanceCents/100)}</strong></div><div><span>Lowest end-of-day balance</span><strong>{money(timing.original.minimumClosingBalance.amountCents/100)}</strong></div><div><span>Extra opening cash to avoid that deficit</span><strong>{money(shortfall)}</strong></div></div>
<h3>Build the situation</h3><p>Start on 12 October 2026 with $400. Receive $2,200 every fortnight from 14 October. Pay $1,300 rent every fortnight from 12 October and $300 living costs every Friday from 16 October. The fixed 91-day window ends on {timing.original.endDate}.</p>
<div class="experiment-table"><table><caption>First week: the order matters</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Money in</th><th scope="col">Money out</th><th scope="col">Closing balance</th></tr></thead><tbody>{timing.original.days.slice(0,7).map(day=><tr><th scope="row">{day.date}</th><td>{money(day.incomeCents/100)}</td><td>{money(day.expenseCents/100)}</td><td>{money(day.closingBalanceCents/100)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Change one thing: move rent to payday</h3><p>Keep every amount the same, but move the rent schedule two days later. The lowest closing balance becomes <strong>{money(timing.changed.minimumClosingBalance.amountCents/100)}</strong>. However, if rent leaves before pay arrives on the same day, the conservative within-day minimum is still <strong>{money(timing.changed.conservativeMinimum.amountCents/100)}</strong>. Matching dates does not guarantee matching transaction times.</p>
<p>The extra {money(shortfall)} is a mathematical minimum for this schedule, not an emergency-fund recommendation. It leaves no allowance for surprises. A payment-date change needs the recipient’s agreement; it is not permission to pay rent late. The model continues below zero to expose the gap and does not simulate a declined payment, overdraft fees, holidays or bank processing delays.</p>
<details><summary>Check the full 13-week reconciliation</summary><p>Opening {money(timing.original.openingBalanceCents/100)} + income {money(timing.original.totalIncomeCents/100)} − expenses {money(timing.original.totalExpenseCents/100)} = closing {money(timing.original.closingBalanceCents/100)}. Changing the rent date keeps the number of payments in this particular window unchanged. Other date changes can move a payment outside the window.</p><div class="experiment-table"><table><caption>Original schedule, week-end balances</caption><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Income</th><th>Expenses</th><th>Closing</th></tr></thead><tbody>{timing.original.weeks.map(w=><tr><th scope="row">{w.weekNumber}</th><td>{money(w.incomeCents/100)}</td><td>{money(w.expenseCents/100)}</td><td>{money(w.closingBalanceCents/100)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>
<p class="experiment-actions"><a href="/tools/cash-flow/">Build your own payday plan →</a><a href="/worked-examples/cash-flow.csv" download="howloop-timing-ledger.csv">Download the 91-day ledger (CSV)</a></p><p class="experiment-source">Background: <a href="https://moneysmart.gov.au/budgeting/budget-planner">ASIC Moneysmart’s budget planner</a>. Our addition is a date-by-date comparison and an explicit same-day ordering check. <a href="/methodology/#cash-flow">Calculation method</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="experiment" id="offers"><p class="experiment-eyebrow">02 / UNPACK THE OFFER</p><h2>When is a $100,000 offer smaller than $95,000?</h2><p class="experiment-intro">When one quote includes employer super and the other adds it on top. Compare the same parts before comparing the headline.</p>
<div class="experiment-table"><table><caption>Annual amounts, assuming standard 12% employer super on all cash salary</caption><thead><tr><th>What you compare</th><th>$100,000 including super</th><th>$95,000 plus super</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Cash salary before tax</th><td>{money(offers.packageOffer.grossAnnual)}</td><td>{money(offers.plusOffer.grossAnnual)}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Employer super</th><td>{money(offers.packageOffer.superAnnual)}</td><td>{money(offers.plusOffer.superAnnual)}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Total package</th><td>{money(offers.packageOffer.packageAnnual)}</td><td>{money(offers.plusOffer.packageAnnual)}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>The dividing line</h3><p>$100,000 ÷ 1.12 = {money(offers.packageOffer.grossAnnual)} cash salary. Subtracting 12% from $100,000 would give $88,000, which is the wrong base: the contribution is a percentage of cash salary, not the whole package.</p><p>The $95,000-plus-super offer has <strong>{money(offers.plusOffer.grossAnnual-offers.packageOffer.grossAnnual)} more annual cash salary before tax</strong>. An including-super offer would need to reach <strong>{money(offers.plusOffer.packageAnnual)}</strong> to match those two components. That does not establish which job is better: hours, leave, commute, bonuses and salary-sacrifice arrangements can change the comparison.</p><h3>Ask before accepting</h3><p>Confirm in writing whether the quote includes super, which earnings attract super, and whether bonuses or other benefits are inside the package. These example salaries are below the standard contribution cap and assume all cash salary qualifies. Super is not spendable take-home pay; no tax, HELP or deductions are subtracted in this table.</p><p class="experiment-actions"><a href="/calculators/salary/">Compare offers and estimated take-home pay →</a></p><p class="experiment-source">Rule reference: <a href="https://www.ato.gov.au/tax-rates-and-codes/key-superannuation-rates-and-thresholds/super-guarantee">ATO super guarantee rates and thresholds</a>. <a href="/methodology/#salary">Model, source dates and exclusions</a>. This example does not advance the source-review date of the payroll dataset.</p></section>
<section class="experiment" id="bonus"><p class="experiment-eyebrow">03 / TEST THE CONDITIONS</p><h2>Does a 5% headline always beat a steady 4%?</h2><p class="experiment-intro">Only if the rate you actually earn makes it better. Here, missing a hypothetical bonus changes that month’s rate from 5% to 1%. Even which months you miss can matter.</p><p>Start with $10,000 and add $200 at each month’s end for 12 months. Compare a steady 4% with a conditional 5% rate. These are invented rates to isolate one question, not live offers. All rates are annual rates, applied monthly.</p>
<form data-bonus-form hidden><label for="missed-months">Months without the bonus</label><select id="missed-months" name="missed">{Array.from({length:13},(_,i)=><option value={i} selected={i===3}>{i} {i===1?'month':'months'}</option>)}</select><button type="submit">Compare</button></form>
<div aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><p data-bonus-summary>Default experiment: 3 missed months. Compare the first 3 months with the last 3 months.</p><div class="experiment-table"><table><caption>One year, equal contributions, before tax and fees</caption><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Final balance</th><th>Interest earned</th><th>Against steady 4%</th></tr></thead><tbody data-bonus-results>{bonus.scenarios.map((s,i)=><tr><th scope="row">{s.label}</th><td>{money(s.balance)}</td><td>{money(s.interest)}</td><td>{i===0?'Baseline':money(s.balance-bonus.scenarios[0].balance)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></div>
<h3>Why averaging the rates misses something</h3><p>Both bonus scenarios have the same number of 1% months. But the balance changes as deposits and interest arrive, so applying a low rate to a later, larger balance can produce a different result. The calculation keeps the order of months, rather than applying a single average rate.</p><details><summary>Reproduce the arithmetic and understand its limits</summary><p>For each month: closing balance = opening balance × (1 + that month’s annual rate ÷ 1,200) + $200. Keep full precision between months; round only the displayed dollars. Total contributions are $12,400, including the starting $10,000. Interest = final balance − $12,400.</p><p>In the early-miss scenario, months 1 through the selected number earn 1%; the rest earn 5%. In the late-miss scenario, the final selected number earn 1%. This is a sensitivity test, not a prediction of your behaviour. A real account may calculate daily, apply balance caps, require spending or growth, change its rates or pay bonuses later. Tax, fees, withdrawals and inflation are excluded. The evidence download contains the default three-miss scenario; it does not change with this control.</p></details><p class="experiment-actions"><a href="/calculators/compound-interest/">Explore your own savings amount and rate →</a></p><p class="experiment-source">Background: <a href="https://moneysmart.gov.au/saving/compound-interest">ASIC Moneysmart on compound interest</a>. Our comparison uses the same savings engine as our calculator, with an explicit monthly rate schedule. <a href="/methodology/#savings">Calculation method</a>.</p></section>
<footer class="experiment-note"><h2>Make the work inspectable.</h2><p>The tables are generated from the calculation functions, rather than typed independently. Our regression checks cover payment timing, package reconciliation and bonus-rate boundary cases. They do not represent professional financial review. <a href="/editorial-policy/">Read our editorial policy</a>.</p><p>Found a result that does not add up? <a href="/contact/">Send the example and the disputed step</a>, using sample amounts. <a href="/updates/">See corrections</a>.</p></footer>
</div>
<script>
import {bonusExperiment} from '../../lib/worked-experiments.mjs';
const form=document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-bonus-form]');
const tbody=document.querySelector('[data-bonus-results]');
const summary=document.querySelector('[data-bonus-summary]');
if(form&&tbody&&summary){form.hidden=false;form.addEventListener('submit',event=>{event.preventDefault();const count=Number(new FormData(form).get('missed'));const result=bonusExperiment(count);const money=(n:number)=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-AU',{style:'currency',currency:'AUD'}).format(n);tbody.replaceChildren(...result.scenarios.map((s,i)=>{const tr=document.createElement('tr');[s.label,money(s.balance),money(s.interest),i===0?'Baseline':money(s.balance-result.scenarios[0].balance)].forEach((value,j)=>{const cell=document.createElement(j===0?'th':'td');if(j===0)cell.setAttribute('scope','row');cell.textContent=value;tr.append(cell);});return tr;}));summary.textContent=`${count} missed ${count===1?'month':'months'}: compare missing the first ${count} with the last ${count}.`;});}
</script>
